<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Quba Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1726 CE – 1806 CE (approx.) · capital Quba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A khanate in north-eastern Azerbaijan whose ruler Fatali Khan united much of the eastern Caucasus in the late 18th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The khanate grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Quba</a> district on the Caspian slope of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Its rulers, from a local family, were recognised by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> in 1726 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, and after Nader's death in 1747 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ali-khan-of-quba">Husayn Ali Khan</a> ruled as an effectively independent khan from his new town of Quba.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-of-quba">Fatali Khan</a>, who ruled from 1758, conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/derbent-khanate">Derbent Khanate</a> in 1765, dominated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/baku-khanate">Baku Khanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/javad-khanate">Javad Khanate</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>, creating the largest state in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. He built a strong administration and army and negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>. The khanate lived from farming, carpets, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and Caspian trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-of-quba">Fatali Khan</a>'s death in 1789, his union fell apart. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-ali-khan-of-quba">Sheikh Ali Khan</a> resisted the Russian advance, but in 1806 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Quba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> and later abolished the khanate.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/quba-khanate.jpg" alt="Quba Khanate" width="320" height="313"><figcaption>Image: Владимир Ильич Томкеев · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khanate_of_Guba_in_the_Map_of_Caucasus_with_the_borders_1801-1813.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1726 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Quba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Husayn Ali Khan, Fatali Khan, Sheikh Ali Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (Shia and Sunni)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani; Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Quba, Kuba Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1766&amp;focus=quba-khanate" aria-label="Quba Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5866 694 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Quba Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6186 950L6224 991M6186 950L6281 1235M6186 950L6487 457M6186 950L6173 937M6186 950L6193 1002"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6224 991h0M6281 1235h0M6487 457h0M6173 937h0M6193 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6186 950h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6186 950h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1766&amp;focus=quba-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

